[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
OSPF and inter-campus routing are healthy; there is no missing route for 172.16.30.0/24. Evidence:
- From the reported source, PC1 (10.5.0.1), end-to-end reachability to VLAN 30 hosts works: pings to 172.16.30.12 and 172.16.30.14 succeed with 0% loss.
- The core and distribution routers all have the OSPF route to 172.16.30.0/24 installed. For example, router16 shows OSPF 172.16.30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2, and routers 7/8/10/11/17 also list OSPF routes to 172.16.30.0/24.
- The only failing test was a ping from PC1 to 172.16.30.1, which returned “Destination Host Unreachable” generated by 10.2.2.2 (the upstream next-hop). That symptom indicates the specific host address .1 is not present/ARP-resolvable on VLAN 30, not that the subnet route is missing.

Mechanism: Because OSPF is advertising 172.16.30.0/24 across the core, traffic to valid hosts in that subnet is delivered. Packets to 172.16.30.1 fail at the last hop due to no host/SVI using that IP.

Localization: No routing/OSPF process or interface fault. The last-hop router toward VLAN 30 (next-hop 10.2.2.2) cannot resolve 172.16.30.1.

Root cause: User tested an unused/nonexistent IP (172.16.30.1) and inferred a routing failure.

Corrective fix: No OSPF changes required. Advise the user to test against valid VLAN 30 addresses (e.g., 172.16.30.12/14) or the gateway 172.16.30.254. If 172.16.30.1 is intended to exist, provision it on the VLAN 30 SVI/host. 
=== END ===